        "kind": "pull_request",
        "title": "fix: route bindings return the wrong agent when route identifiers contain separators",
        "body": "## What Problem This Solves\n\nFixes an issue where routes that use distinct peer, guild, or role identifiers can return the wrong agent when an identifier contains a cache-key separator. The second route can reuse a cached result from a different routing context.\n\n## Why This Change Was Made\n\nRoute cache inputs are encoded as a structured JSON array so each field and role-array boundary remains distinct. The change preserves the existing order-insensitive role semantics while preventing delimiter-based collisions. This cache is process-local only: it is a `WeakMap` keyed by the active configuration object, so the change does not alter persisted state or require an upgrade migration.\n\n## User Impact\n\nOperators can safely route messages using identifiers that contain tabs, commas, or hyphens without another route's cached result being reused.\n\n## Evidence\n\n- Added regression coverage for peer/guild tab-delimiter collisions and role-ID comma-delimiter collisions.\n- `node scripts/run-vitest.mjs src/routing/resolve-route.test.ts` — 56 tests passed.\n- `pnpm format:check -- src/routing/resolve-route.ts src/routing/resolve-route.test.ts` — passed.\n- Isolated runtime routing proof was run with the PR source using one in-memory config and verbose resolver logs. Four consecutive calls selected distinct expected agents:\n  ```text\n  [proof] peer-tab -> agent=whole-peer matchedBy=binding.peer\n  [proof] peer-guild -> agent=guild-room matchedBy=binding.peer\n  [proof] role-comma -> agent=comma-role matchedBy=binding.guild+roles\n  [proof] role-suffix -> agent=suffix-role matchedBy=binding.guild+roles\n  ```\n  The run used synthetic identifiers only and no external channel credentials or messages.\n\n## AI-assisted\n\nThis PR was prepared with AI assistance. The author reviewed the behavior, implementation, and validation results.",
